enchilada: GPU: a page-table pool for the whole client range, and the range ends at L1[2] (K5)

The PTE pool was fixed at 64 L3 tables (GPU_PTE_TABLES, 2 MiB of GPU VA each, 128 MiB) and
mapped with ml_io_map(), while the iova allocator hands out the whole range up to 0xc001ffff: the
first BO past 0x88000000 found no table (the 0195 panic). gpu_bo_init() now takes as many tables
as the sweetfw3 blob holds, up to 511 (L2[1..511]; L2[0] has the payload's own L3), i.e. every
2 MiB chunk of the range. More than 64 tables do not go through ml_io_map(): io_submap is 8 MiB
for every device mapping and kmem_alloc() panics when it is full (0194's analysis). The pool lies
right behind the arena, below topOfKernelData, so it gets the static aperture alias exactly like
the arena (the page checks of 0194 move into gpu_aperture_check() and are shared), only when the
arena's own aperture passed (so sweetarena=0 also keeps 64 ml_io_map tables). That alias is
cacheable and the SMMU walks the tables uncached (TCR IRGN0/ORGN0 = 0), so every store into a
pool table - zeroing a new table, a PTE in vm_map and vm_unmap - is cleaned to memory by
physical address before the barrier and the TLB invalidate. A 64-table blob, or no aperture,
gives exactly the old pool. The per-table L2-index list (a linear scan per PTE) becomes a
512-entry L2-index -> table map. GPU_VA_BYTES now ends the range at 0xc0000000: an iova in
0xc0000000..0xc001ffff is L1[3], but gpu_l3_for() only looks at the L2 index, so it would have
rewritten the PTE of 0x80000000 + the same offset; MSM_PARAM_VA_SIZE says 0x3ffe0000 instead
of 0x40000000. The boot line says how many tables, how many MiB that maps and which alias.

